---
import { applyResponseDefaults } from '~/helpers/astro/request.js'

import Layout from '~/src/layouts/default.astro'
import LegalPage from '~/src/components/legal-page.astro'
import { dataRecipients } from '~/helpers/data-recipients.js'
import { INFO_PLIST_ALLOWED_KEYS, MAX_VALUE_LENGTH } from '~/helpers/scan-allowlist.js'

// One row per key in helpers/scan-allowlist.js, so this table cannot drift from what the scanner sends.
const keyDescriptions = {
    CFBundleIdentifier: 'The app\'s bundle ID, for example com.tinyspeck.slackmacgap',
    CFBundleName: 'The app\'s short name',
    CFBundleDisplayName: 'The app\'s display name',
    CFBundleShortVersionString: 'The app\'s version, for example 4.41',
    CFBundleVersion: 'The app\'s build number',
    CFBundlePackageType: 'The bundle type, for example APPL for an app',
    LSMinimumSystemVersion: 'The oldest macOS version the app supports',
    LSMinimumSystemVersionByArchitecture: 'The oldest macOS version per processor type (only when it is a single text value)',
    LSArchitecturePriority: 'Which processor type the app prefers (only when it is a single text value)',
    LSRequiresNativeExecution: 'Whether the app refuses to run under Rosetta 2',
    NSHumanReadableCopyright: 'The app\'s copyright notice',
    DTSDKName: 'The macOS SDK the app was built with',
    DTXcode: 'The Xcode version the app was built with',
}

const collectedKeys = INFO_PLIST_ALLOWED_KEYS.map( key => [ key, keyDescriptions[ key ] || '' ] )

applyResponseDefaults( Astro )
---
<Layout
    headOptions={ {
        title: 'Privacy Policy - Does It ARM',
        description: 'What Does It ARM collects on the website, App Scanner, email lists and MCP server, why, who handles it, and your rights.',
        pathname: '/privacy'
    } }
>
    <LegalPage title="Privacy Policy" effective="November 27, 2026">

        <p>Does It ARM is a free site that tracks which Mac apps run natively on Apple Silicon. It is run by Sam Carlton Creative LLC, an Oklahoma limited liability company, doing business as Does It ARM ("we", "us"). This policy covers doesitarm.com, the App Scanner, our email lists and the Does It ARM MCP server at mcp.doesitarm.com. It explains what we collect, why, who handles it, and your choices.</p>

        <p>The short version: there are no accounts. We collect little, and we never sell your personal data. Questions go to <a href="mailto:hello@doesitarm.com">hello@doesitarm.com</a>.</p>

        <h2>What we collect and why</h2>

        <h3>Visiting the website</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Hosting logs.</strong> The site runs on Cloudflare, and a few data functions run on Vercel. Like any web host, they process your IP address, browser user agent and the pages you request so they can deliver the site and protect it from abuse. Their logs are kept under their own policies.</li>
            <li><strong>Analytics and ads.</strong> We measure visits and service offer interactions, test offer headlines, and show ads on some pages. The table below describes the providers and what they receive.</li>
            <li><strong>Search.</strong> Site search runs in your browser. Your search terms are not sent to us.</li>
            <li><strong>Affiliate links.</strong> If you follow an affiliate link and make a purchase, we may earn a commission. The destination may set its own cookies.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In the EU, the rest of the European Economic Area, the UK and Switzerland, analytics and ad scripts stay off until you select <strong>Accept</strong> in the cookie banner. We apply the same rule when we can't determine your region. <strong>Reject</strong> keeps them off. Your choice is saved in your browser's local storage. Use <strong>Cookie settings</strong> in the footer to change it at any time.</p>

        <h3>Email signups</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>App updates newsletter.</strong> When you sign up in the site footer, we store your email address, when you signed up and where on the site, in a Cloudflare database. We send the newsletter through Kit (kit.com). We pass your address to Kit, which sends you a confirmation email first and handles unsubscribes and bounces.</li>
            <li><strong>App alerts and waitlists (where offered).</strong> If you sign up for alerts about an app or for a waitlist, we store your email address, what you signed up for and the page you used. You must confirm by clicking a link in an email before we send you anything else. Unconfirmed signups are deleted after about 72 hours. These emails are sent through Cloudflare Email Sending.</li>
            <li><strong>Service requests.</strong> If you use a form on our services pages, we store your email address, the offer, any app and details you enter, and the page you used, so we can reply.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>To slow down spam, signups are rate limited using your IP address and email address. These limits are checked with hashed values and are not saved with your signup. Every email we send has an unsubscribe link, or you can email us to unsubscribe.</p>

        <h3>The App Scanner</h3>
        <p>The App Scanner checks apps in your browser. <strong>Your app files are never uploaded.</strong> We do not collect the app's files, its full Info.plist, the contents of its program files, or file and folder paths on your Mac.</p>

        <p>When a scan is shared with us, it contains only these details:</p>
        <table class="legal-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Detail</th><th scope="col">What it is</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                { collectedKeys.map( ([ key, description ]) => (
                    <tr><td><code>{ key }</code></td><td>{ description }</td></tr>
                )) }
                <tr><td><code>architectures</code></td><td>The processor types the app's main program is built for (arm64, arm64e, x86_64, x86_64h or i386), read from its Mach-O header</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>result</code></td><td>Whether the scan found native Apple Silicon support</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>appVersion</code>, <code>filename</code></td><td>The app's version and the app's name with no folder path, for example "Slack.app", so we can match scans of the same app</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>The first {collectedKeys.length} details come from the app's Info.plist, the standard settings file inside every Mac app. We keep only text, number and true/false values, each cut to {MAX_VALUE_LENGTH} characters, and drop anything else. We do not collect any other Info.plist keys, such as update feed URLs, nor Mach-O load commands, code signing data or other metadata. In return for a scan, we tell you if we know of a version of that app that runs natively.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>The current scanner.</strong> The default App Scanner sends the details above every time you scan an app. It has no setting to turn this off. It will be replaced by the newer scanner described below. If you do not want to share, use the newer scanner with sharing turned off.</li>
            <li><strong>The newer scanner.</strong> Where offered, the newer App Scanner has an unchecked checkbox, “Also check for Apple Silicon upgrades and download links”. Only if you check it, the scanner sends the details listed above for each app so we can return a newer compatible version and link, and those details are added to Does It ARM's compatibility data under our <a href="/terms">Terms</a>. Unchecked, nothing is sent.</li>
            <li><strong>Where it goes.</strong> Scan reports are stored by a function on Vercel in a Google Sheet. We do not store your name, an account or your IP address with a scan report. Older scan reports in that sheet, sent before November 27, 2026, include more detail (the full Info.plist and Mach-O metadata). They stay private and are no longer published.</li>
            <li><strong>What we publish.</strong> An app's public Does It ARM page and our public data files show only a few fields from scan reports: the app version, the bundle ID, the processor types (shown as Universal, Apple Silicon or Intel), the minimum macOS version and the scan result.</li>
            <li><strong>Scanning a URL.</strong> If you scan a download link, our servers or your browser fetch that public URL to read the app's details.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Why we collect app details.</strong> App details help us keep the public compatibility list accurate and find newer versions of an app that run natively. We have a legitimate interest in this, and it benefits everyone who uses the list. You can object at any time: use the newer scanner with the setting turned off, scan offline, or email us to ask us to remove a report.</p>
        <p><strong>Public apps only.</strong> We want details about publicly available apps, not internal or confidential ones. Please do not scan apps that contain confidential information, such as in-house company apps, while sharing is on. We may delete scan data that looks like it comes from a non-public app.</p>

        <h3>The MCP server and inventory API</h3>
        <p>The Does It ARM MCP server lets AI assistants look up compatibility data. The REST inventory endpoint at <code>mcp.doesitarm.com/v1/inventory</code> provides the same inventory check over HTTP. Both are read-only and require no account.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>We do not store, cache, or log what you send to the MCP tools or REST inventory endpoint, including raw export files. We use device IDs or labels only to count devices in the current request. Device details are removed from results.</li>
            <li>Your IP address is used briefly to limit MCP and REST traffic to a combined 120 requests a minute per IP address. It is not stored by us.</li>
            <li>Cloudflare, which runs the server, processes standard request data under its own policies.</li>
            <li>The AI app you use with the server, such as Claude or ChatGPT, has its own privacy policy.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Emailing us and contributing</h3>
        <p>If you email us, we keep the conversation so we can refer back to it. If you suggest an app on GitHub, your post is public on GitHub and GitHub's privacy policy applies.</p>

        <h2 id="data-recipients">Who we share data with</h2>
        <div class="recipient-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="data-recipients">
            <table class="legal-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Recipient</th>
                        <th scope="col">Purpose and data</th>
                        <th scope="col">Cookies and EU consent</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    { dataRecipients.map( recipient => (
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row">
                                <a href={ recipient.privacyPolicy }>{ recipient.name }<span class="sr-only"> privacy policy</span></a>
                                <span class="surfaces">{ recipient.surfaces.join( ', ' ) }</span>
                            </th>
                            <td>{ recipient.purpose } { recipient.data }</td>
                            <td><strong class="category">{ recipient.consentCategory }</strong>{ recipient.cookies } { recipient.euConsent }</td>
                        </tr>
                    ) ) }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>We may also share information if the law requires it, to protect Does It ARM or its users from abuse, or as part of a transfer if Does It ARM changes hands. In that last case, this policy would continue to apply to data collected under it. The compatibility list itself is public, and anyone may use it.</p>
        <p><strong>We do not sell your personal data.</strong></p>

        <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Newsletter: while you are subscribed. After you unsubscribe, we may keep your address marked as unsubscribed so we do not email you again. Ask us if you want it deleted completely.</li>
            <li>Unconfirmed alert signups: about 72 hours.</li>
            <li>Service requests and emails: as long as we need them to reply and keep a record, or until you ask us to delete them.</li>
            <li>Scan reports: as long as they are useful to the public compatibility list.</li>
            <li>Hosting logs and analytics: as set by each provider's retention settings.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Your rights</h2>
        <p>Wherever you live, you can ask us to tell you what personal data we have about you, to correct it, to delete it, or to stop using it. Email <a href="mailto:hello@doesitarm.com">hello@doesitarm.com</a>. We may need to confirm the request comes from you, for example by replying from the email address involved.</p>
        <p>If you are in the European Economic Area, the UK or Switzerland, you can access, correct, delete, restrict, object to and move your personal data, and complain to your data protection authority. We rely on your consent for analytics and ads in these regions and for the newsletter and alerts. You can withdraw it at any time. We rely on our legitimate interests to run and protect the site and to collect app details from the scanner. You have the right to object to that, as described above.</p>

        <h2>Children</h2>
        <p>Does It ARM is not directed at children under 13 (or under 16 in the EEA and UK). We do not knowingly collect their personal data. If you think a child has given us personal data, email us and we will delete it.</p>

        <h2>Where data is processed</h2>
        <p>Does It ARM is run from the United States. Our providers may process data in the United States and in other countries where they operate.</p>

        <h2>Security</h2>
        <p>The site uses HTTPS everywhere, and we collect as little as we can. No system is perfectly secure, but we work to protect what we have.</p>

        <h2>Changes</h2>
        <p>We will update this policy when our practices change and refresh the date at the top. For significant changes, we will also take other reasonable steps to let people know. This page's full history is public on GitHub.</p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>Sam Carlton Creative LLC, doing business as Does It ARM. Email <a href="mailto:hello@doesitarm.com">hello@doesitarm.com</a>. Also see our <a href="/terms">Terms of Use</a>.</p>

    </LegalPage>
</Layout>

<style>
    .recipient-table {
        max-width: 100%;
        overflow-x: auto;
        margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }
    .recipient-table:focus-visible {
        outline: 2px solid #fff;
        outline-offset: 2px;
    }
    .recipient-table .legal-table {
        display: table;
        min-width: 35rem;
        margin-bottom: 0;
    }
    .recipient-table th[scope="row"] {
        position: sticky;
        inset-inline-start: 0;
        width: 9rem;
        background: #171a21;
        padding-inline-start: 0.5rem;
    }
    .surfaces {
        display: block;
        margin-top: 0.25rem;
        color: #d1d5db;
        font-weight: normal;
    }
    .category {
        display: block;
        text-transform: capitalize;
    }
</style>
